---
import CaseStudyLayout from '../../../layouts/CaseStudyLayout.astro';
import PhoneLink from '../../../components/ui/PhoneLink.astro';
import WhatsAppCTA from '../../../components/ui/WhatsAppCTA';
import { siteConfig } from '../../../config/site';

const { phone, url } = siteConfig;

const breadcrumbs = [
  { name: 'The Counting House', url: `${url}/case-studies/counting-house` },
  { name: 'Timber Matching', url: `${url}/case-studies/counting-house/timber-matching` },
];

const articleProps = {
  headline: 'The Counting House — Timber Matching & Finishing',
  url: `${url}/case-studies/counting-house/timber-matching`,
  datePublished: '2024-01-01',
};
---

<CaseStudyLayout
  title="Counting House Timber Matching"
  description="Profile-matched timber, blended finishes, and period-accurate ageing on a Pontefract listed building, so repair sections sit naturally with the original fabric."
  breadcrumbs={breadcrumbs}
  articleProps={articleProps}
>
  <section class="py-16 lg:py-24 bg-paper">
    <div class="max-w-7xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8">
      <p class="text-oak uppercase tracking-wide mb-4">The Counting House — Pontefract</p>
      <h1 class="font-display text-4xl lg:text-5xl text-ink mb-6">Timber Matching &amp; Finishing</h1>
      <p class="text-xl text-ink/70 max-w-2xl">
        Profile matching, colour blending, and period-accurate finishing across repaired timber elements, so new sections sit naturally with the original building fabric.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="py-16 bg-bone">
    <div class="max-w-5xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8 space-y-10">
      <div>
        <h2 class="font-display text-2xl lg:text-3xl text-ink mb-4">Why matching matters</h2>
        <p class="text-ink/70 mb-3">
          A technically correct repair that reads as a bright new insert undermines the building and the work. On a listed building, where original character matters, obvious new sections jar. The aim is for the repair to sit in the building naturally - not to deceive, but to maintain the coherence of the original fabric.
        </p>
        <p class="text-ink/70">
          Achieving this requires attention at two levels: profile matching in the joinery, and finish matching in the applied layers. Both were applied consistently across The Counting House project.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div>
        <h2 class="font-display text-2xl lg:text-3xl text-ink mb-4">Profile matching</h2>
        <p class="text-ink/70 mb-3">
          Period timber joinery has moulded profiles - ovolo beads on sash members, bolection and stuck mouldings on door rails and stiles, weathered and throated profiles on external cills and weatherboards. These were machined to specific profiles when the building was constructed and are not standard modern sections.
        </p>
        <p class="text-ink/70 mb-3">
          At The Counting House, measurements were taken from undamaged sections of each element before cutting the repair timber. New timber was machined to match the profiles, section dimensions, and grain direction. Where profiles were complex, test sections were cut and checked against the original before the repair timber was machined to final dimensions.
        </p>
        <p class="text-ink/70">
          The result is that the section join is in the right place structurally and the profile reads continuously across the repair. Profile mismatch - where a repair has been made with the nearest standard section rather than the correct one - is one of the most common and most visible failings in period timber repair.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div>
        <h2 class="font-display text-2xl lg:text-3xl text-ink mb-4">Finish matching - the process</h2>
        <p class="text-ink/70 mb-3">
          The existing finish on The Counting House joinery was identified before any matching work began. Paint systems on exterior joinery, oil finishes on exposed internal timber, and the build-up of decades of maintenance products were all factors in the matching approach.
        </p>
        <p class="text-ink/70 mb-3">
          Test boards were cut from the same timber species and section as the repair pieces. Stain concentrations, oil systems, and primer compatibility were adjusted until the test board matched the adjacent original in natural light - not under artificial light, where the assessment is misleading.
        </p>
        <p class="text-ink/70">
          The new timber was also surface-prepared before finishing: wire-brushed lightly to open the grain and remove the freshness from cut surfaces, edges slightly softened by hand-planing to remove the sharpness that distinguishes new from old. Then the matched finish system was applied before the repair was assembled into the building.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div>
        <h2 class="font-display text-2xl lg:text-3xl text-ink mb-4">What we do not use</h2>
        <p class="text-ink/70 mb-3">
          Polyurethane varnish is not used on heritage or listed timber joinery. It sits as a film on the surface, traps moisture beneath it on exterior work, and often fails as peeling or cracking that requires stripping before re-finishing. Once polyurethane has been applied to old joinery and failed, the re-finishing process is significantly more disruptive than if a compatible penetrating finish had been used from the start.
        </p>
        <p class="text-ink/70">
          The finish systems used at The Counting House - oil and paint systems appropriate to the substrate and location - are compatible with the breathable character of period construction and avoid creating future stripping problems.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div>
        <h2 class="font-display text-2xl lg:text-3xl text-ink mb-4">The result</h2>
        <p class="text-ink/70 mb-3">
          The repair sections across windows, doors, and frames at The Counting House sit in the building without drawing the eye. In natural light, from normal viewing distances, the joins are not obvious. Up close and in direct light, they are visible - that is the honest position. No matching work is invisible on day one.
        </p>
        <p class="text-ink/70">
          Over twelve to twenty-four months, as the new timber weathers and the finish layers settle, the remaining visual difference reduces further. This is expected and normal. The important thing is that the repair was done correctly and that the finish system applied is compatible with the existing building fabric.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="flex flex-wrap gap-6 pt-4 border-t border-ink/10">
        <a href="/case-studies/counting-house" class="text-oak font-display">← Back to Counting House</a>
        <a href="/services/finishing" class="text-oak font-display">Finishing service →</a>
        <a href="/services/timber-repair" class="text-oak font-display">Timber Repair &amp; Restoration →</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="py-16 bg-paper">
    <div class="max-w-7xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8 text-center">
      <h2 class="font-display text-3xl text-ink mb-4">Need a repair that blends in?</h2>
      <p class="text-ink/70 mb-8 max-w-2xl mx-auto">Send photos of the timber and the surrounding fabric. Rob approaches matching from the material outward, not from a colour chart.</p>
      <div class="flex flex-wrap justify-center gap-4">
        <WhatsAppCTA client:load />
        <PhoneLink phone={phone.tel} display={phone.display} variant="secondary" />
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</CaseStudyLayout>
